@meta
id: 12-09
title: Look without leaving your place
chapter: 12
type: lesson
skills: search-slash
par: 58
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl+F to peek, Escape, scroll back to where you were
vimrc: set incsearch
open: refund.sweep.task.ts

@brief
The publish call in `run` has an empty queue name. The right value is the
string held in `REFUND_QUEUE`, declared at the bottom of the file, and it
has to go in literally — the queue name is not imported here.

So this is the one job a find box is genuinely for: go and look at
something, then come back and carry on. Vim can do that, and the trick is
that a search you never finish costs you nothing. While you are typing a
pattern, vim is already showing you the match; if you abandon the search
instead of confirming it, the cursor has not moved at all.

Put the queue name into the empty quotes and save.

@keys
| `/text` | with `incsearch` on, the view jumps to the match as you type |
| `Esc` | abandon the search — the cursor stays exactly where it was |
| `Ctrl-C` | the same thing, if `Esc` is awkward |
| `Enter` | confirm the search and actually move there |
| `ci'` | change what is inside the quotes |

@hints
Read the constant first, then edit. You do not need to travel to the
bottom of the file and come back — a search shows you the line while you
are still typing it.
--
Start a search for `REFUND_QUEUE` and watch the screen. With `incsearch`
on, the match is on screen before you press Enter. Read the value, then
press `Esc` rather than `Enter`: the search is abandoned and the cursor
never left the top of the file.
--
Then `/queue:` `Enter` to land on the empty pair of quotes, and `ci'`
with the string you just read.

@solution
/REFUND_QUEUE<Esc>  /queue:<Enter>  ci'payments.refunds.v2<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

`incsearch` makes vim show you the match while you are still typing the
pattern. It is worth having on for two reasons. It tells you immediately
when a pattern is wrong — no match, no jump — and it turns a search into
a peek: look at the screen, press `Esc`, and nothing moved.

That is the honest answer to "but sometimes I really do just want to
look". Ctrl+F in VS Code is a looking tool that leaves your cursor behind.
`/` with `Esc` is the same thing, and `/` with `Enter` is the version that
takes you there. One key decides which you meant, at the end, once you
have seen the match.

Two smaller things while a search prompt is open. The command line has a
history: press the up arrow and your previous searches come back one by
one, filtered by whatever you have typed so far. Vim's own answer to
retyping a long pattern is an arrow key, which is funny given everything
this dojo says about arrow keys — so use `Ctrl-P` and `Ctrl-N` instead.
They walk the same history without your hand leaving the home row, and
without the keystroke log noticing.

And an empty pattern reuses the last one: `/` `Enter` searches again for
whatever you last searched for, which is occasionally clearer than `n`
when you have been moving around in between.

@start refund.sweep.task.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { Task } from '../../infrastructure/task';
import { RefundProvider } from '../../domain/refund/refund.provider';
import { QueueProducer } from '../../infrastructure/queue.producer';

@injectable()
export class RefundSweepTask extends Task {
  constructor(
    @inject(RefundProvider) protected refundProvider: RefundProvider,
    @inject(QueueProducer) protected queueProducer: QueueProducer,
  ) {
    super();
  }

  public schedule = '0 */2 * * *';

  public async run() {
    const pending = await this.refundProvider.findAwaitingApproval();

    for (const refund of pending) {
      await this.queueProducer.publish({
        queue: '',
        payload: { refundId: refund.id, orderId: refund.orderId },
      });
    }

    return { published: pending.length };
  }

  public async onFailure(error: Error) {
    await this.queueProducer.publish({
      queue: 'payments.deadletter.v1',
      payload: { task: 'RefundSweepTask', message: error.message },
    });
  }
}

export const REFUND_SWEEP_BATCH = 200;

export const REFUND_SWEEP_LOCK = 'lock:refund-sweep';

export const REFUND_QUEUE = 'payments.refunds.v2';

export const REFUND_QUEUE_DLQ = 'payments.refunds.v2.dead';

